const history = `SELECT order_id, placed_at, status
FROM orders
WHERE customer_id = 1
ORDER BY placed_at DESC, order_id DESC
LIMIT 10;`;
---

<p>A slow application request does not tell you that its SQL needs a faster plan. It may be waiting to borrow a connection, waiting for a lock after reaching the database, or competing for a processor or storage with other work. Each can leave the same spinning page in front of the customer. A useful diagnosis connects that symptom to the work or dependency holding it up.</p>

<p>We will follow Mina asking the shop for her order history. Order 103 already exists, pending, with its two lines worth 29.00 and no payment. Her ordinary history read changes nothing. Separate branches introduce a pool queue, a PostgreSQL table lock and shared resource pressure; they are alternatives, not one accumulating incident. The drawings and their timings are illustrative.</p>

<p>The aim is to choose one action whose effect you can predict and check. Native evidence and intervention commands belong to <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/operations/">Operating and diagnosing PostgreSQL</a>; the same reasoning needs the appropriate evidence on another engine.</p>

<section aria-labelledby="start-request">
<h2 id="start-request">Start with the request</h2>

<p>Mina should receive her newest orders. The small starting records return 103, pending, followed by 101, paid. The query asks for the order explicitly:</p>
<Sql code={history} label="Mina’s order-history read" />

<p>Suppose the page now takes a second where comparable requests previously completed promptly. Begin with the request’s boundaries: when it arrived, when it asked for a connection, when that connection was acquired, when the statement was sent and when the result was consumed. Figure 1 separates those intervals. Watch how much of the request happens before any SQL can be sent.</p>
<RequestTime />

<p>The 600 ms acquisition wait accounts for most of this invented request. Making its statement instantaneous would still leave at least those 600 ms. Conversely, a trace that wraps the whole connection call in a span named “database” can make a pool queue look like slow execution. Record what each interval actually includes.</p>

<p>The request’s total already contains its statement’s time. Adding both would count the statement twice. The same applies inside the database: a statement’s elapsed time can contain lock waits, storage waits and result delivery. A long elapsed time is a place to investigate, not a processor-use measurement.</p>

<h3 id="comparable-work">Compare the same work before and after</h3>
<p>Establish the incident window and the affected work. Are all history requests slow, only one customer’s, or every endpoint using the same pool? Count arrivals, successful useful completions and errors as well as latency. An average for successful requests can improve while more callers time out or are refused.</p>

<p>Keep a small evidence window spanning the onset: request traces with parameters handled appropriately, pool measurements, database activity and wait samples, resource trends and the times of releases, migrations and report starts. Compare equivalent inputs and traffic. Mina’s ten-row history lookup and a year-long report are different work even when they read the same tables.</p>

<p>A release just before the slowdown is a candidate explanation. It becomes stronger when it accounts for a changed query rate, a longer transaction or the actual blocking operation. Timing alone does not tell you which. A missing database reply also establishes only what the caller heard; in a writing request it would not establish whether a commit happened.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="pool-delay">
<h2 id="pool-delay">Waiting for a connection</h2>

<p>A connection pool lends a limited set of database conversations to application work. A caller waiting to borrow one has not sent its statement on that connection. Correlate its acquisition interval with the number of borrowed clients, available clients and queued callers in that same pool. A quiet database cannot show a statement the application has not sent.</p>

<p>Then follow the borrowers. A client can stay occupied while its query executes, waits for a database lock, delivers a large result or waits for application code to finish a transaction. Code can also forget to return it. A full pool establishes where the later callers wait; it does not yet explain why the earlier borrowers remain there. Check each application instance’s pool rather than assuming one total describes them all.</p>

<p>Figure 2 starts with a separate session holding a table lock. Step through until the two later callers arrive, then follow their dependency back to that session. Notice which callers have actually reached the database.</p>
<LockChain />

<p>The first three callers borrowed clients and reached PostgreSQL. The two later callers did neither. Both groups can report slow history requests, but only the first group has a current database statement to inspect. A request identifier, connection identity and statement interval let you connect those observations without inventing five database waiters.</p>

<p>Increasing this pool’s limit would let more history statements reach the same lock. It would not release H. Under resource pressure it could instead admit more competing work. First explain what keeps clients occupied; <a href="/learn/databases/relational/connections-and-capacity/">Connections, overload and capacity</a> develops the limits, queue budgets and overload behaviour after that diagnosis.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="database-waits">
<h2 id="database-waits">Waiting inside the database</h2>

<p>Once a request has a connection and an outstanding statement, match that statement to its database session and sample what it is waiting for. A session described as active need not be using a processor. In PostgreSQL, activity state and wait information answer different questions: a query can be active while blocked.</p>

<p>The lock branch uses an <code>ACCESS EXCLUSIVE</code> lock on <code>orders</code>. Mina’s ordinary <code>SELECT</code> needs a conflicting table lock, so it waits. An ordinary row-update lock would not explain this read waiting: PostgreSQL’s ordinary snapshot reads do not wait for those row locks. The kind of lock and the requested operation have to fit the explanation.</p>

<p>H’s lock statement has finished, but its transaction is still open. That is why looking only for a long-running current statement can miss the cause. Follow the blocked statement to its blocker, then inspect that transaction’s lifetime, owner and remaining work. If the blocker also waits, follow the next edge. A chain ending at an idle holder has no cycle; it need not be a deadlock and can outlast a deadlock detector’s checking interval.</p>

<p>Figure 2’s queued caller leaving does not affect the three lock waiters. Cancelling one of those database waiters would end that selected statement, but would still leave H holding the lock. The useful intervention depends on who can release the resource, not simply which waiting request looks oldest.</p>

<p>For an actual incident, retain the statement identity, requested resource, blocking relationship and transaction state together. One sample can catch a brief wait that explains little of a long request; repeated samples and the request’s interval make the case stronger. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/locks-and-deadlocks/">Locks, contention and deadlocks</a> develops repairs to the protected interval, lock scope and acquisition order. Native monitoring and selected cancellation remain with the operations article.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="expensive-execution">
<h2 id="expensive-execution">When execution is expensive</h2>

<p>If acquisition is prompt and the statement spends its time doing work rather than waiting for the identified lock, investigate that work. The shop’s paid-order report adds agreed line values for paid orders in October and groups them by customer. Its two-row teaching result, Tariq 40.00 and Mina 39.50, says little about the work of finding those totals in a large history.</p>

<p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-execution/">Query execution and performance</a> follows that report through scans, joins, repeated line lookups, grouping and sorting. Here the incident question comes first: did its work grow, did the same work start happening more often, or did it begin overlapping with foreground requests?</p>

<div class="incident-table" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Evidence for growing execution work">
<table>
<caption>Follow the change within the incident window</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Possible change</th><th scope="col">Evidence to compare</th><th scope="col">What it would explain</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">More input</th><td>Parameters and rows examined per execution</td><td>A wider reporting range does more work even with the same plan.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">More repetition</th><td>Call counts and overlapping executions</td><td>An acceptable report can consume a shared resource when run repeatedly.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">A changed route</th><td>Plans for comparable inputs, including repeated inner work</td><td>Each invocation now performs more searches or carries more rows onwards.</td></tr>
