<p>Use changes in cumulative counters over the window, checking their reset times and coverage. A statement with the largest lifetime total may have been cheap throughout this incident. A single slow sample can be unusual, and a quick isolated execution does not reproduce the traffic competing with Mina.</p>

<p>Connect statement work to resource use in the same period: processor demand, storage activity, temporary work and memory pressure. Absence of a recorded wait does not by itself prove processor saturation. A measured plan can locate the work, but collecting one with <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> runs the statement again. An overloaded service may not have room for that experiment. Preserve existing evidence and use a suitable copy or a bounded diagnostic run.</p>

<p>Pausing an optional reporting schedule is a testable containment when the evidence points there. Predict that new report executions stop, resource demand falls and history completions improve. Already running reports require their own decision; switching off their scheduler does not cancel them. Plan analysis and testing a lasting SQL or index repair belong to the execution article.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="storage-pressure">
<h2 id="storage-pressure">Storage delay and running out of space</h2>

<p>Storage pressure has two different forms. A statement can wait too long for a read, a temporary write or a durable log flush while plenty of space remains. A volume can also run out of space while its latency still looks acceptable. Measure latency and available space separately.</p>

<p>For delay, match the affected operation’s waits with storage activity and latency during the incident. Database page visits include cached work; a page reported as read by PostgreSQL can still come from the operating system’s cache. A full buffer pool is normal cache use, not proof that the drive is saturated. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage-and-durability/">Storage and durability</a> follows those boundaries.</p>

<p>Other work can share the same device: reports reading pages or spilling, background page writes, and log writes needed by transactions. Find which operation and which storage location account for the change. More demand on a shared volume can delay a small history read whose own plan has barely changed.</p>

<p>For space, examine the relevant volumes, quotas and growth trends, then identify what is growing: data, indexes, temporary files, retained row versions, recovery logs or unrelated files. Figure 3 shows an invented trajectory. Compare the remaining space with the proposed repair’s peak requirement, not just with zero.</p>
<Space />

<p>At minute ten, “four GiB left” sounds less urgent than “the six-GiB repair no longer fits”. The estimate must also leave room for ongoing writes and cleanup. Extrapolating the recent slope gives a warning horizon, not a promise: a new report or retained log can change it abruptly.</p>

<p>Deleting rows need not return their file space to the operating system. Rebuilding a table can require another copy and take an excluding lock; on PostgreSQL, <code>VACUUM FULL</code> does both. A near-full volume is therefore a poor place to start an unbudgeted rewrite. Do not remove database or recovery-log files by hand. Use the operations, <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/">vacuum</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/recovery/">WAL</a> procedures to establish safe relief. A full WAL volume can shut PostgreSQL down, so preserving recovery headroom is part of containment.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="bounded-intervention">
<h2 id="bounded-intervention">Choose a bounded intervention</h2>

<p>Turn the explanation into one action with a predicted effect, a scope and a stopping condition. For Figure 2, the evidence identifies H as a lock-only transaction with no business changes to preserve. Its owner can end it with rollback. The scope is that transaction; the first predicted effect is release of the table lock, followed by the readers finishing and clients returning.</p>

<p>Choose an observation period long enough to see those stages, bounded by the service’s remaining deadline and risk. If H has ended but the expected lock waits remain, check the current blockers. If the lock waits disappear but the queue keeps growing, follow the remaining execution or result-consumption delay. Do not broaden the action merely because the first graph has not returned to normal.</p>

<p>For the report branch, the bounded action might be to stop new optional report runs and observe the foreground workload while existing work finishes. Record when it took effect and what actually stopped. If the supported resource demand falls without history recovery, the report was not the whole explanation. Keep the evidence and revise the hypothesis.</p>

<h3 id="cancellation-recovery">Cancellation and recovery still do work</h3>

<p>A caller giving up, a statement being cancelled and a session ending are different events. An application deadline does not prove a cancellation reached the database. A cancellation request does not prove the statement has already stopped. Confirm its terminal result and the transaction’s state before reusing the client; a failed transaction must be ended cleanly.</p>

<p>Ending a session discards its connection and aborts unfinished transaction work. It can therefore affect more than the statement currently visible. Recovery costs depend on the engine and the work: InnoDB can spend substantial time undoing a large transaction, while PostgreSQL hides aborted row versions and reclaims their space later. Neither promises that every resource becomes available the instant a cancellation is requested.</p>

<p>A lost connection during commit leaves a separate uncertainty. The transaction might already have committed. Preserve its request identity and reconcile through the operation’s recovery protocol; do not turn a missing reply into a new purchase. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/conflicts-and-retries/">Retries and uncertain commit outcomes</a> develops that distinction.</p>

<p>A restart has a wider scope again. It interrupts other sessions, can trigger crash recovery, loses the database’s warm cache and may bring a rush of reconnecting clients and retries. A restart that removes today’s holder can still leave tomorrow’s workload producing the same queue. Estimate that recovery work before using it as relief.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="live-change">
<h2 id="live-change">When a live change triggered the incident</h2>

<p>A schema operation may explain the table exclusion in the lock branch. A backfill, which gradually writes existing rows into a new form, may instead explain increased execution, page writing or log growth. Correlate the particular change’s state with the waits or resource demand; “a migration is running” does not identify which mechanism is delaying Mina.</p>

<p>Pausing the migration launcher does not establish that its current database operation ended. Reverting the application release does not undo committed backfill work or prove that older callers still accept the current schema and values. Contain the demonstrated pressure, preserve the change’s state, and check which callers and database operations remain active.</p>

<p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/schema-changes/">Live schema changes and backfills</a> develops the compatible sequence of schema, data and caller states, including when a change can safely pause or return. During the incident, that compatibility is a condition on the proposed intervention, not something a healthier latency graph can establish.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="verify-recovery">
<h2 id="verify-recovery">Verify that useful service recovered</h2>

<p>The lock release is only the first predicted change. Figure 4 follows it back to Mina. Read each arrow as a check still to make.</p>
<Recovery />

<p>Observe useful completions, errors, latency and queue drainage under the relevant arrivals. Check requests already queued as well as newly arriving ones. A quiet database after refusing every caller is not a recovered history service. Nor does one successful probe establish that the original request mix is healthy.</p>

<p>Restore paused reporting gradually within the available headroom and watch whether the cause returns. If foreground service degrades again, retain the bound while the lasting repair is tested. Record the incident window, supported cause, intervention, predicted effects and observed recovery so that the next owner can reproduce the reasoning.</p>

<p>The lasting work follows what you found: shorter protected intervals or a different lock protocol, less query work, explicit admission budgets, storage relief or a compatible change procedure. The diagnosis is complete enough to act when it explains both where Mina waited and what had to change for her request to finish.</p>
</section>

